---
import danHeadshot from "../images/headshots/dan-studio-portrait.png";
import marinaHeadshot from "../images/headshots/marina-studio-mauve.png";
import kyleHeadshot from "../images/headshots/kyle-studio-sage.png";
import Avatar from "../components/Avatar.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import { proof } from "../data/services";
---

<Layout
  title="About | 17th Street Labs"
  description="17th Street Labs is an applied AI engineering studio in Denver, New York City, and Boston. Meet the engineers who do the work."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="chip">About</p>
      <h1 class="statement">We don't sell AI theater.</h1>
      <div class="lede messaging-copy">
        <p>We build things. We benchmark them. We break them.</p>
        <p>We measure what happened. We fix them. Then we try to break them again.</p>
        <p>17th Street Labs works in the messy territory between:</p>
        <p><strong>“Holy shit, this is possible.”</strong></p>
        <p>and</p>
        <p><strong>“Yes, we can trust this in the real world.”</strong></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <dl class="proof">
        {proof.map(({ value, label }) => (
          <div>
            <dt>{value}</dt>
            <dd>{label}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="team">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band-head">
        <p class="chip">Team</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Senior people, one studio.</h2>
        <p>The people you meet are the people who do the work.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="founders">
        <article class="card founder">
          <Avatar src={marinaHeadshot} alt="Marina Levy" />
          <div>
            <h3>Marina Levy</h3>
            <p class="role">Co-founder, chief executive officer</p>
            <p class="bio">
              Marina runs the business behind the engineering: operations,
              partnerships, sales, and media. She knows what it takes to get an
              ambitious idea off the ground—and keep it moving. Building a
              venture that led to a $10 million exit in Tokyo and two companies
              in Morocco taught her what execution demands. Reviewing pitches
              and helping deploy capital with New York City investors sharpened
              her eye for which ideas deserve it. That same instinct led her to
              found{" "}
              <a href="https://skillriseeducation.com/about" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">SkillRise Education</a>,
              a woman-led edtech company preparing people for the AI economy.
            </p>
            <a class="text-link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/marina-a-solovyov" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Marina on LinkedIn <Icon name="ArrowRight" size={16} /></a>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="card founder">
          <Avatar src={danHeadshot} alt="Dan Levy" />
          <div>
            <h3>Dan Levy</h3>
            <p class="role">Co-founder, founding engineer</p>
            <p class="bio">
              Some of the engineers companies compete to hire started in Dan’s
              classroom. Across Lambda School and Galvanize, he helped more than
              3,000 students build their skills—including beginners who went on
              to careers at leading companies. That changes a life, not just a résumé.
              His own spans 25+ years building production software, including
              roles at Hotel Engine and MagicSchool. At 17th Street Labs, he
              brings that depth to the hard AI problems—and helps your team
              understand the solution well enough to take it further.
            </p>
            <a class="text-link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/realdaniellevy" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Dan Levy on LinkedIn <Icon name="ArrowRight" size={16} /></a>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="card founder">
          <Avatar src={kyleHeadshot} alt="Kyle" />
          <div>
            <h3>Kyle Coberly</h3>
            <p class="role">Head of Product Engineering</p>
            <p class="bio">
              Kyle connects the engineering, the economics, and the people who
              have to use the thing. At 17th Street Labs, he leads product
              engineering: deciding what’s worth building, making it work at
              scale, and helping your team take it from there.
            </p>
            <p class="bio">
              He’s helped 1,000 people change careers into software engineering.
              He brings that educator’s instinct to his work at the National
              Society of Leadership and Success, his consulting firm Sika
              Education, and the{" "}
              <a href="https://professionalstudies.du.edu/instructors/kyle-coberly/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">University of Denver</a>,
              where he teaches information systems as an Adjunct Assistant
              Professor. Before that, he was Faculty Director at Galvanize,
              working alongside Dan, and Director of Education at Flatiron
              School. He builds the product—and the team’s ability to own it.
            </p>
            <a class="text-link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/kylecoberly/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Kyle on LinkedIn <Icon name="ArrowRight" size={16} /></a>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band-head">
        <p class="chip">Principles</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Ambitious and rigorous, at the same time.</h2>
        <p>
          AI should be part of the product, not a chatbot bolted on. And none
          of it matters if the thing does not work. We hold both.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="principles">
        <div class="card">
          <span class="card-icon"><Icon name="Gauge" /></span>
          <h3>Stop guessing. Start measuring.</h3>
          <p>
            “Seems better” is not a methodology. We measure what changed,
            find the failure, fix it, and prove the fix worked.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <span class="card-icon"><Icon name="ShieldCheck" /></span>
          <h3>The system is the product.</h3>
          <p>
            The model is one component. Architecture, harness, context, and
            tools decide the outcome. The best design often uses a smaller
            model.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <span class="card-icon"><Icon name="Zap" /></span>
          <h3>We experiment on ourselves first.</h3>
          <p>
            We invest our own capital in R&amp;D, testing reliability,
            performance, and cost before bringing an approach into your business.
            You get the benefit of that research—and the knowledge to run
            what we build.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="closing">
        <h2 class="statement">Put it in front of engineers.</h2>
        <p>Tell us what you’re working on. We’ll tell you what we would test first.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-solid" href="/contact/">
            Start a project
            <Icon name="ArrowRight" size={16} />
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
